        <body>
            <div xml:id="d69">

                <pb facs="0114" n="106" sameAs="frus:frus1938v01/pg106"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.62/483: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Rumania</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gunther</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bucharest</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-11-30T12:00:00+02:00">November 30, 1938—12
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 30—6:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>141. Dr. M. W. Fodor, Central European correspondent of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Chicago Daily News</hi> tells me that he feels satisfied
                    that a non-aggression pact has recently been privately offered by Germany to
                    Russia. His information is that the latter has not yet answered but he
                    interprets the recent Russo-Polish declarations and the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Izvestia</hi> article urging closer collaboration with us as indirect
                    replies à la Russe.</p>
                <p>May I invite your attention to Fodor’s current series of articles in the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Chicago Daily News</hi> on German economic penetration
                    in the Balkans.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gunther</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
